# The kama compiler kyoten builds with: .kama-pin/ (git-ignored), made from a CLEAN clone of kama at the commit below,
# the binary beside the lib/, include/ and prelude/ it was built with. tools/kama runs it and warns when the compiler
# it runs is another. AGENTS.kyoten.md § Build has the recipe; a bump is a commit of its own, made with every suite
# green on the new compiler and nothing else changed.
#
# Windows (msys2 UCRT64), 2026-11-08: first pin, 0.9.886 (2159ce21), at the owner's direction that kyoten keep its own
# copy because ../kama rebuilds as it develops on this machine (it moved 0.9.877 -> 0.9.881 -> 0.9.884 in one session).
# Before it, steps 4 to 5.2 were built on ../kama's working binary: 0.9.876, then 0.9.881+g4267d2ec.
#
# 2026-11-09: 0.9.886 -> 0.9.899 (f32373ea), kama's dev HEAD with a clean tree. What moved, for kyoten:
#   0.9.893 KR-357 (KY-41, KY-42): isize and usize at the target's width at compile time.
#   0.9.894 KR-358 (KY-46): `T.default()` is an operand, so `Id.second() != Id.default()` builds.
#   0.9.895 KR-359 (KY-43): the strict reader refuses a key the object already gave, naming its path.
#   0.9.896 (KY-47): InlineDynamicArray's length is an int32, one size on every target.
#   0.9.897 KR-360 (KY-39, KY-44): a query names each type's declaration (`path`), says whether a symbol is
#           `exported`, and reads an elected ctor as `default ctor`.
#   Also 0.9.887-0.9.892 (Xtensa atomics, a wasm program that spawns compiles with -pthread, parallel_for on the job
#   pool, a sub-int operation beside an unsuffixed literal takes the operand's type) and 0.9.898-0.9.899 (fibers on
#   Windows), none of which kyoten uses. Green on it with no other change: tools/test.sh (engine 114 cases native debug,
#   release and wasm; the frame guard; the tool's 117; fps native and wasm; vr checked), @kama/window's (36 native,
#   20 under node) and @kama/gpu's (3 cases, the clear example's 60 frames, the xfail, the wasm build).
#
# 2026-11-10: 0.9.899 -> 0.9.909 (64f011de), kama's dev HEAD with a clean tree. kama had rebased dev twice since the
# last pin, so f32373ea is on no branch of ../kama any more (its rebased 0.9.899 twin, e3d58e7f, has another tree) and a
# clean clone could not rebuild it; the old binary is kept in .scratch/kama-pin-0.9.899. What moved, for kyoten:
#   0.9.905 KR-364 (KY-48), first half, BREAKING: `JsonDeserializer.make(src:, strict:)` replaces `strict(src:)`, and
#           `deserializeJsonBuffer` takes `strict:`. The bump carries the port, the one change "nothing else changed"
#           could not hold off: 12 `.strict(src: x)` calls became `make(src: x, strict: true)`, and 3
#           `deserializeJsonBuffer` calls, which were lenient, state `strict: false`. Behavior is unchanged.
#   0.9.907 KR-364 (KY-48), second half: `JsonValue.reader(strict:)`, a reader over a parsed tree.
#   0.9.909 KR-365 (KY-49): `kama query --modules a,b` roots a project's query at those modules.
#   Also 0.9.900-0.9.903 (fibers on Windows), 0.9.904 (a job pool hang), 0.9.906 (a `match` subject through a raw
#   pointer) and 0.9.908 (KR-366: `PriorityQueue` and `Descriptor` constructors), none of which kyoten uses.
#   Green on it with the port alone, the same counts as on 0.9.899 the same morning: tools/test.sh (engine 158 cases
#   native debug, release and wasm; the frame guard; the tool's 188; `kyoten check engine/kama.json`; fps native and
#   wasm; vr checked), @kama/window's (36 native, 20 under node) and @kama/gpu's (3 cases, the clear example's 60
#   frames, the xfail, the wasm build).
#
# 2026-11-10: 0.9.909 -> 0.9.911 (e76900bf), kama's dev HEAD with a clean tree; 64f011de is still on its history, and
# the 0.9.909 binary is kept in .scratch/kama-pin-0.9.909. What moved, for kyoten:
#   0.9.911 KR-367, BREAKING: std's constructors are named by how they make the value (SPEC § Construction: `make` from
#           parts, `of` only memberwise, `empty`/`with<Setting>`, `from<Input>`, `parse(text:)`, an OS verb or a noun;
#           never `new`, `create`, `init`, `of<Input>` or `fromStr`). The bump carries the port, the one change
#           "nothing else changed" could not hold off: 2 `IoError.of(kind:)` calls became `IoError.make(kind:)` (the
#           engine's content tests and the tool's `spin` fixture), and in @kama/gpu's examples/clear
#           `parse::<int32>(s: v)` became `int32.parse(text: v)`. Behavior is unchanged.
#   Also 0.9.910 (a diagnostic for a `spawn` refused inside its scope) and a KR-3 test fixture, neither of which kyoten
#   uses. Green on it with the port alone, the same counts as on 0.9.909 the same morning: tools/test.sh (engine 164
#   cases native debug, release and wasm; the frame guard; the tool's 192; `kyoten check engine/kama.json`; fps native
#   and wasm; vr checked), @kama/window's (36 native, 20 under node) and @kama/gpu's (45 cases, the clear example's 60
#   frames, the xfail, the wasm build).
#
# 2026-11-10, the same evening: rebuilt at 238198b3. kama rebased its dev within hours, so e76900bf is on no branch of
# ../kama any more and a clean clone could not rebuild it. Its twin, 238198b3 (0.9.911, the same author date), differs
# only in docs/grammar.bnf and tools/check-reserved-hint.sh, neither of which the compiler is built from, and the two
# builds' lib/ and prelude/ are identical. The e76900bf binary is kept in .scratch/kama-pin-0.9.911-e76900bf. Green
# on the rebuild with nothing else changed: tools/test.sh, @kama/window's (38 native, 22 under node, after its new
# case) and @kama/gpu's (45 cases, 60 frames, the xfail, the wasm build, COVERAGE.md).
#
# 2026-11-12: 0.9.911 -> 0.9.928 (94b8025b), kama's dev HEAD with a clean tree; 238198b3 is still on its history, and
# the 0.9.911 binary is kept in .scratch/kama-pin-0.9.911. Decided by the owner on 2026-11-12, once kama had fixed the
# two gaps kyoten's fifteenth session found. What moved, for kyoten:
#   0.9.913 and 0.9.915 KR-370, BREAKING: a primitive has no constructors of its own, so `int32.parse(text:)` is refused,
#           and a contract's member is named with its contract: `Parseable::<int32>.parse(text: t)`, SPEC's one
#           spelling. The bump carries the port, the one change "nothing else changed" could not hold off: in
#           @kama/gpu's examples/clear, `int32.parse(text: v)` became `Parseable::<int32>.parse(text: v)`.
#   KR-377 (deb11208, then 732ead84): `kama run --target WASM` runs the page in a browser (KY-50).
#   KR-380 (fdd09083): a program named by a relative path with `/` starts on Windows when the current directory is not
#           searched (KY-51; its repro, .scratch/ky51-spawn, passes on this pin).
#   KR-379 (94b8025b): an extern value's InlineArray field is a kama array in every use, so writing one no longer fails
#           in C (@kama/window's KWIN-1; its repro, extended to an element write, a whole write and a whole read, passes).
#   Also 0.9.912 (a cross-isolate wake on Linux), 0.9.914 (adapters onto declared types), 0.9.916 (a park's fast path),
#   0.9.917-0.9.918 (`Instant` counts only time awake), 0.9.919-0.9.922 (Android: debuggable apps, resources and icon,
#   App Bundles, several ABIs in one package) and 0.9.923 (the per-user directories and `TempDir`), none of which kyoten
#   uses. Green on it with the port alone, the same counts as on 0.9.911 the same morning: tools/test.sh (engine 164
#   cases native debug, release and wasm; the frame guard; the tool's 192; `kyoten check engine/kama.json`; fps native
#   and wasm; vr checked), @kama/window's (249 native debug and release, 163 under node, 264 in each of Chrome for
#   Testing 157 and Edge) and @kama/gpu's (96 native debug and release, the clear example's 60 frames, the xfail, 74 in
#   each browser and 60 frames on its canvas).
#
# 2026-11-12: 0.9.928 -> 0.9.938 (94365be5), kama's dev HEAD with a clean tree; 94b8025b is still on its history, and
# the 0.9.928 binary is kept in .scratch/kama-pin-0.9.928. Decided by the owner on 2026-11-12 (the eighteenth session),
# once kama had answered the two gaps the sixteenth session filed. What moved, for kyoten:
#   43f45f5a: `kama run --target WASM --scale <n> --window <w>x<h>`, a browser run's device scale and window size
#           (KY-52; its repro, .scratch/ky52-scale, prints devicePixelRatio 1.5 in Edge 157 and Firefox 160 with
#           `--scale 1.5`, and 1 without).
#   090da76f, 94365be5: a browser that never asks for the page ends the run within 60 s, naming the browser and quoting
#           what it said (KY-53; the unzipped Chrome for Testing 157 is reported after 62 s with its "Network service
#           crashed", exit 2, where it waited silently).
#   0b7a2bb0, 8b4bb117, a99a4729: a browser that ends first says why, and its temporary files and the run's directory
#           go once every process it started has gone.
#   Also 52e718ee (a Linux build fix), 867e3707 (a macOS run's .dSYM), 6d17820c (KR-376, the debugger guard), 63aa78eb
#   (the fixture watchdog), 5d48a965 (Apple's entropy source), f9dc4f77 (KR-381, a kama library linked into a C host)
#   and addd14cd (KR-281's probes, a UIKit app on the iPad), none of which kyoten uses yet. Green on it with nothing
#   else changed, the same counts as on 0.9.928 the same evening: tools/test.sh (engine 164 cases native debug,
#   release and wasm; the frame guard; the tool's 192; `kyoten check engine/kama.json`; fps native and wasm; vr
#   checked), @kama/window's (249 native debug and release, 163 under node, 245 under `kama run` in Edge and Firefox,
#   264 through the driver in Chrome for Testing) and @kama/gpu's (109 native debug and release, the clear example's
#   60 frames, the xfail, 88 in Edge and in Chrome for Testing, 18 in Firefox, 60 frames on each canvas).
KAMA_VERSION=0.9.938
KAMA_BUILD=g94365be5
